1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Sol Solar answers a household before sunrise. Will the battery last the night?

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Kyiv, a 5 kilowatt array, tilt 35, facing south. Battery size, charge, and house load are typed. Nothing is read from a meter.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:33,042
battery_through_the_night. The battery should last until sunrise. The forecast sun is not enough to refill it. Today is about 1.3 kilowatt-hours.

4
00:00:33,042 --> 00:00:47,083
best_time_for_appliance. Wait until tomorrow. Run the washing machine from 1 PM to 3 PM. That 0.7 kilowatt wash is about 1.4 kilowatt-hours.

5
00:00:47,083 --> 00:01:01,083
plan_the_solar_day. The washer fits tomorrow. The dishwasher and the car charging do not. An hour is shared only when the roof can cover both.

6
00:01:01,083 --> 00:01:15,083
daily_solar_briefing. The strongest stretch today is 1 PM to 4 PM, about 0.6 kilowatt-hours. That is a thin window for heavy loads.

7
00:01:15,083 --> 00:01:26,083
The browser is an MCP client. initialize negotiates protocol 2025-11-25 over Streamable HTTP. The sentence is the tool's text.

8
00:01:26,083 --> 00:01:38,083
tools/list from the official MCP client. Protocol 2025-11-25. Seven tools, including the night-battery check and the day plan.

9
00:01:38,083 --> 00:01:52,083
npm test. 18 tests pass and 0 fail. The suite stays offline. It does not call the live forecast.

10
00:01:52,083 --> 00:02:02,083
Open-Meteo and PVGIS. October 2026, Vadym Bilobrovets. Alexa+ linking needs the partner preview. This is the MCP client the FAQ accepts.
